@node Main "Riffwright"

@{b}Riffwright@{ub} - a ProTracker music editor for AmigaOS 4

Riffwright loads, edits, plays and saves ProTracker modules (.mod). It is written in
Boing BASIC: the editor is BASIC code, the player is the runtime's MOD player.

  @{" Getting started    " link Start}
  @{" The screen         " link Screen}
  @{" Pattern editor     " link Pattern}
  @{" Entering notes     " link Notes}
  @{" Effects            " link Effects}
  @{" Blocks             " link Blocks}
  @{" Order list         " link Orders}
  @{" Sample editor      " link Samples}
  @{" Song settings      " link Song}
  @{" Playing            " link Playing}
  @{" Files              " link Files}
  @{" ARexx              " link ARexx}
  @{" All keys           " link Keys}
  @{" About              " link About}
@endnode

@node Start "Getting started"
@{b}Getting started@{ub}

Start Riffwright from its icon, or drop a .mod on its window or icon. From a Shell:

  Riffwright [song.mod] [--play]

A new song has four synthesised instruments (square, saw, triangle, noise), so the piano
keys make a sound straight away.

  1. Press @{b}Space@{ub} to switch on edit mode (the EDIT light turns red).
  2. Play notes on the computer keyboard: the bottom row Z S X D C V ... is one octave,
     the top row Q 2 W 3 E R ... the next.
  3. @{b}F5@{ub} plays the song, @{b}F6@{ub} the pattern, @{b}Esc@{ub} stops.
  4. @{b}Amiga+S@{ub} saves.

Two demo songs are in the demos drawer: harbour_lights.mod and boing_shuffle.mod.
@endnode

@node Screen "The screen"
@{b}The screen@{ub}

Top: the song's title and file. Below it, on the left, the counters: position in the
order list and its length, the pattern, the row, speed, tempo (BPM), the octave and the
current sample. The lights show edit mode, follow mode and what is playing.

On the right are the channel scopes, with a VU meter in each. A muted channel's scope is
red.

The middle shows one of three views:
  @{" Pattern editor " link Pattern} (Amiga+1) with the order and sample lists beside it
  @{" Sample editor  " link Samples} (Amiga+2)
  @{" Song settings  " link Song} (Amiga+3)
F10 steps through them. The line at the bottom shows messages and the main keys.
@endnode

@node Pattern "Pattern editor"
@{b}Pattern editor@{ub}

A pattern has 64 rows. Each channel shows the note, the sample number (hex) and the effect
(command and parameter, hex): @{b}C-3 01 C40@{ub}. Rows 0, 4, 8... are numbered in amber, the
16-row bars have a lighter stripe. The cursor row stays in the middle of the view and
the pattern scrolls past it.

  Cursor keys        move (Left/Right go through the fields of each channel)
  Tab / Shift+Tab    next / previous channel
  Alt+Up / Alt+Down  16 rows
  Home / End         first / last row
  Space              edit mode on/off (off = jam: notes play but don't go in)
  Ctrl+1..8          mute a channel; Ctrl+Shift+1..8 solo it (again: all on)

In edit mode:
  Del                clear the field under the cursor (note, sample or effect)
  Shift+Del          clear the whole cell
  Backspace          delete the row above the cursor in this channel (Shift: all)
  Return             insert an empty row here in this channel (Shift: all)
  keypad *           edit step: how many rows the cursor moves after a note (0-8)
@endnode

@node Notes "Entering notes"
@{b}Entering notes@{ub}

The keyboard is a piano. With octave 2:

  Q 2 W 3 E R 5 T 6 Y 7 U I 9 O 0 P [ = ]     C-3 C#3 D-3 ... up to G-4
  Z S X D C V G B H N J M , L . ; /           C-2 C#2 D-2 ... up to E-3

F1 / F2 change the octave (1-3), F3 / F4 (or keypad - / +) the sample. A note gets the
current sample. ProTracker's range is C-1 to B-3; notes above it are refused. The
@{b}1@{ub} key (or `) clears the note under the cursor.

In the sample and effect fields the keys are hex digits (0-9, A-F). A number is typed left
to right, then the cursor goes down by the edit step.

Without edit mode the keys only play the notes: try a tune before writing it down.
@endnode

@node Effects "Effects"
@{b}Effects@{ub}

The ProTracker commands (the parameter is hex xy):

  0xy arpeggio          1xx slide up           2xx slide down
  3xx tone portamento   4xy vibrato            5xy portamento + volume slide
  6xy vibrato + volume slide                   7xy tremolo
  9xx sample offset     Axy volume slide       Bxx jump to position
  Cxx volume (00-40)    Dxx break to row (decimal) of the next position
  Exy extended: E1 fine slide up, E2 fine down, E5 finetune, E6 loop, E9 retrigger,
      EA/EB fine volume, EC cut, ED delay, EE pattern delay
  Fxx speed (01-1F) or tempo (20-FF)
@endnode

@node Blocks "Blocks"
@{b}Blocks@{ub}

Shift+cursor keys select a block. Without a selection the block commands work on the
whole channel under the cursor.

  Amiga+C   copy                 Amiga+X   cut
  Amiga+V   paste at the cursor  Amiga+B   mix paste (keeps what is under empty fields)
  Amiga+A   select the channel   Amiga+Shift+A  select the whole pattern
  Amiga+T   transpose up a semitone, Amiga+Shift+T down, with Alt: an octave
  Amiga+I   interpolate effect parameters from the first row to the last (slides)
  Amiga+K   set the notes in the block to the current sample
  Amiga+D   duplicate the pattern into a new one at the next position
  Amiga+P   a new empty pattern at the next position
  Amiga+Del clear the pattern
  Amiga+Z   undo (200 steps)     Amiga+Shift+Z or Amiga+Y  redo
@endnode

@node Orders "Order list"
@{b}Order list@{ub}

The order list says which pattern plays at each position of the song. In the pattern view:

  Ctrl+Left / Right   previous / next position (and show its pattern)
  Ctrl+Up / Down      change the pattern at this position

The song view has the whole list as a grid: type two digits for a pattern number, Ins or
Return inserts a position, Del deletes one.
@endnode

@node Samples "Sample editor"
@{b}Sample editor@{ub} (Amiga+2)

The waveform of the current sample with its loop (amber lines) and, while it plays, the
play position (green).

  Up / Down           choose a setting: name, volume, finetune, loop start, loop length
  Left / Right        change it (Shift: 16 times as much)
  Return              edit the name
  + / -               zoom in / out; Alt+Left / Right scroll
  L                   load a sample: IFF 8SVX (also Fibonacci-delta packed), WAV (8 or
                      16 bit, mono or stereo; faster than 22 kHz is resampled to 16574 Hz,
                      the rate at which C-3 plays it at its own pitch), or raw 8-bit data
  W                   save it: name it .wav, .raw or anything else for 8SVX
  R                   reverse     N  normalise     X  cut to the loop end
  Del                 clear the sample
  piano keys          play it
@endnode

@node Song "Song settings"
@{b}Song settings@{ub} (Amiga+3)

Title, speed (ticks per row at the start), tempo (BPM at the start), song length and the
restart position. Up / Down choose, Left / Right change (Shift: 10), Return edits the title.
@endnode

@node Playing "Playing"
@{b}Playing@{ub}

  F5        play the song from the current position
  F6        play the current pattern (loops)
  F7        play the song from the cursor row
  Esc / F8  stop
  F9        follow mode on/off: the view follows the song while it plays

With follow mode and edit mode on, notes go in at the row being played: record live.
Edits are heard at once while the song plays.
@endnode

@node Files "Files"
@{b}Files@{ub}

  Amiga+O   open a module           Amiga+S  save (Amiga+Shift+S: save as)
  Amiga+N   new song                Amiga+Q  quit

Riffwright reads 31-sample ProTracker modules with 4 channels (M.K., M!K!, FLT4, 4CHN), and
6 and 8 channels (6CHN, 8CHN, FLT8, OCTA). A loaded file saves back byte for byte when it
was not changed, including data after the last sample.
@endnode

@node ARexx "ARexx"
@{b}ARexx@{ub}

The port is @{b}RIFFWRIGHT@{ub}. Commands:

  PLAY [position]   play the song
  STOP              stop
  LOAD file         load a module
  SAVE [file]       save (to its own file without an argument)
  POSITION [n]      go to position n; returns the position being played

Example:
  address RIFFWRIGHT
  'LOAD demos/harbour_lights.mod'
  'PLAY'
@endnode

@node Keys "All keys"
@{b}All keys@{ub}

  F1/F2 octave    F3/F4 sample    F5 play song   F6 play pattern   F7 play from row
  F8/Esc stop     F9 follow       F10 next view  Help this guide
  Amiga+1/2/3 pattern/samples/song
  Amiga+O/S/N/Q open/save/new/quit, Amiga+Z/Y undo/redo
  Amiga+C/X/V/B copy/cut/paste/mix, Amiga+A select, Amiga+T transpose,
  Amiga+I interpolate, Amiga+K set sample
  Ctrl+Left/Right position, Ctrl+Up/Down pattern at position, Ctrl+1..8 mute
@endnode

@node About "About"
@{b}About Riffwright@{ub}

Riffwright is an example program of Boing BASIC, a Visual Basic 6 style language for
AmigaOS 4. Its demo songs and their synthesised samples were made for it and are free to
use for anything (CC0).
@endnode
